            <h5>Abstract</h5>
            <p> 
              Zero-shot singing voice synthesis (ZS-SVS) aims to generate singing voices for unseen singers from musical scores and audio prompts. A key challenge is learning text-audio alignment that respects note timing without phoneme-level duration annotations. To address this challenge, we propose X-Singer 2, a score-aware audio-infilling framework for multilingual ZS-SVS. We introduce a Musical Score Diffusion Transformer that incorporates note timing into both alignment guidance and positional representation in cross-attention. A score-derived alignment prior guides token-frame correspondences using local note-group intervals. Additionally, note-length-aware rotary positional embedding derives token positions from each note-group's onset and duration. These mechanisms support alignment learning without prescribing phoneme boundaries or requiring an external text-audio aligner. We train our model for audio infilling using conditional flow matching with an x-prediction parameterization. During inference, chained classifier-free guidance adjusts the contributions of text, note, and audio prompt conditions. Experiments demonstrate that X-Singer 2 achieves annotation-efficient and high-quality multilingual SVS for seen and unseen singers.
